  "id": "Compound::DB00443 - Disease::DOID:4481",
  "verbalization": "Betamethasone, a therapeutic glucocorticoid, is implicated in the treatment of Asthma and Allergic Rhinitis through a mechanistic pathway that involves immunomodulatory effects. The evidence suggests that Betamethasone is used to treat Asthma, which in turn is associated with Neurogenic Inflammation, a process that contributes to Allergic Rhinitis. This pathway is supported by the shared lowest common ancestor between Asthma and Allergic Rhinitis, indicating a biological link through inflammatory mechanisms. The presence of Betamethasone in the treatment of Asthma, which is a known mediator of Allergic Rhinitis, implies a potential role in modulating inflammatory responses. The evidence also highlights the relationship between Betamethasone and Methylprednisolone, another glucocorticoid, further reinforcing its role in immunomodulation. The integration of these pathways suggests that Betamethasone may exert its effects through a mechanism that reduces inflammation, potentially alleviating symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis. The observed scores and shared ontological relationships support a mechanistic link, though the exact molecular pathways remain to be fully elucidated.",
